e as the result of a finished run. This rule governs a case **admitted** into this workflow; a fault that exits at Step 1 item 2a to Direct Work is not admitted and writes no record at all.\n7. **A terminal outcome is never overwritten.** Once Step 2b, Step 4 or Step 7 sets `not confirmed`, `no action justified` or `transferred`, the run goes to Step 5's terminal path, then Step 8 and Step 9. No later step advances it to `resolved`.","replace_all":false}},{"tool_name":"Edit","tool_use_id":"toolu_0118Jb9zqVe424GjwKssQnak","tool_input":{"file_path":"/Users/patrik.lindeberg/Claude Code/Axcion AI Repo/ai-resources-diagnostics-workflow/.claude/commands/resolve-incident.md","old_string":"6. **Status is mandatory.** Every incident record carries one of: `diagnosing` / `implementing` / `verifying` / `resolved` / `escalated` / `deferred`. This is how an interrupted session resumes from the record rather than from chat context.","new_string":"6. **Status is mandatory for every admitted case.** Every incident record carries either a transitional **record state** — `diagnosing` / `implementing` / `verifying` — or a terminal **case outcome** — `resolved` / `escalated` / `deferred` / `not confirmed` / `no action justified` / `transferred`. This is how an interrupted session resumes from the record rather than from chat context. The three transitional states are not peer outcomes: never report one as the result of a finished run. This rule governs a case **admitted** into this workflow; a fault that exits at Step 1 item 2a to Direct Work is not admitted and writes no record at all.\n7. **A terminal outcome is never overwritten.** Once Step 2b, Step 4 or Step 7 sets `not confirmed`, `no action justified` or `transferred`, the run goes to Step 5's terminal path, then Step 8 and Step 9. No later step advances it to `resolved`.","replace_all":false}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I have to stop here and ask you for one thing.\n\n**Where we are.** I resolved the Work Loop core, read the state file, and checked the brief's premises against the repository. Every premise holds, including the one I was most worried about: the brief demands real command invocations, not grep checks, and I proved that works — a headless `claude -p '/smoketest ...'` run inside a throwaway fixture executed the command body and wrote its output file there. So the verification contract is achievable.\n\n**The blocker.** The harness refuses my edits to `.claude/commands/`. Two attempts on `.claude/commands/resolve-incident.md` both returned \"you haven't granted it yet\". This is not the filesystem and not your settings — the file is writable on disk, and `.claude/settings.json` has `defaultMode: bypassPermissions` plus an explicit `Edit(**/.claude/**)` allow rule. I confirmed the block is specific to that folder: the same kind of edit succeeded on `docs/repo-architecture.md` and `templates/incident-log-template.md`.\n\nThose two command files are where four of the nine required behaviours live, so I cannot finish the unit without them.\n\n**What I did not do.** I did not use a Bash `cat >` to write the files instead. That would be doing the exact thing the permission layer just refused, through a different door, and it is your call, not mine.\n\n**What is on disk now** — two small edits, uncommitted, both part of the required lockstep:\n- `docs/repo-architecture.md:239` — the incident-log status list now names all six terminal outcomes.\n- `templates/incident-log-template.md:3` — same enumeration in the record template.\n\nI committed nothing. The status vocabulary is half-changed right now, so committing would leave the repo inconsistent.\n\n**What I need from you.** Approve writes to `.claude/commands/` in this session — the permission dialog should appear when I retry. Say the word and I retry immediately.\n\n**One cost warning before you do.** [COST] The brief's verification contract asks for six behavioural cases, each with a negative control, each run as a real `/resolve-incident` or `/resolve-repo-problem` invocation. `/resolve-incident` declares `model: opus` and runs nine steps. That is roughly twelve full headless Opus sessions against disposable fixtures. It is what the brief demands and it is the honest way to test this, but it is a large spend. Tell me if you want me to run the full set or bring a reduced set back to Codex as a stated limitation.","ttft_ms":2053,"ttft_stream_ms":1340,"time_to_request_ms":33,"type":"result","duration_ms":390308,"uuid":"3609b418-17de-4fa8-b436-5f420cec6da5"}
